NNOOTTEESS
       These functions are provided for applications that must ensure that a
       thread’s stack is placed in a particular location.  For most
       applications, this is not necessary, and the use of these functions
       should be avoided.  (Use pptthhrreeaadd__aattttrr__sseettssttaacckkssiizzee(3) if an application
       simply requires a stack size other than the default.)

       When an application employs pptthhrreeaadd__aattttrr__sseettssttaacckk(), it takes over the
       responsibility of allocating the stack.  Any guard size value that was
       set using pptthhrreeaadd__aattttrr__sseettgguuaarrddssiizzee(3) is ignored.  If deemed
       necessary, it is the application’s responsibility to allocate a guard
       area (one or more pages protected against reading and writing) to
       handle the possibility of stack overflow.

       The address specified in _s_t_a_c_k_a_d_d_r should be suitably aligned: for full
       portability, align it on a page boundary (_s_y_s_c_o_n_f_(___S_C___P_A_G_E_S_I_Z_E_)).
       ppoossiixx__mmeemmaalliiggnn(3) may be useful for allocation.  Probably, _s_t_a_c_k_s_i_z_e
       should also be a multiple of the system page size.

       If _a_t_t_r is used to create multiple threads, then the caller must change
       the stack address attribute between calls to pptthhrreeaadd__ccrreeaattee(3);
       otherwise, the threads will attempt to use the same memory area for
       their stacks, and chaos will ensue.

